WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:07.980
Over 200 claims were denied by the insurer,&nbsp;
even though it had been collecting premiums.&nbsp;

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:11.100
The US Department of Labor has accused Prudential&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:14.280
Financial of illegally denying&nbsp;
over 200 life insurance claims,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:18.240
even after the insurer had continued to&nbsp;
collect premiums from the policyholder.

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:23.760
This is only the most recent instance of a rash of&nbsp;
insurers denying claims customers have paid for.&nbsp;

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:27.900
A number of life insurance companies&nbsp;
have been found to be denying claims&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:29.820
made on policies that have been paid for years.

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:33.120
Many employers include supplemental life plans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:37.200
as a part of their employee benefits&nbsp;
packages. This makes it possible for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:40.440
employees to pay an additional premium&nbsp;
for the coverage out of their paychecks.

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:45.420
Prudential of Newark, New Jersey has reached a&nbsp;
settlement with the US government with respect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:49.800
to what is called “evidence of insurability.&nbsp;
” This is a common underwriting practice for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:54.180
supplemental life contracts. It requires&nbsp;
an employee to complete a form that shows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:57.360
that they are in good health before the&nbsp;
start of the employer-sponsored coverage.

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:02.580
Life insurance premiums have been paid by many&nbsp;
employees for years without completing the form.&nbsp;

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:07.080
The settlement detailed how Prudential had&nbsp;
been collecting premiums for some of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:10.200
participants in these employee&nbsp;
plans for years – in some cases&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:14.640
since 2004 – even though that evidence of&nbsp;
insurability form had never been completed.

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:19.260
Prudential had been denying any&nbsp;
claims between 2017 and 2020 that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:22.020
did not have the evidence of the&nbsp;
insurability form completed even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:25.200
though it still collected premiums from&nbsp;
the customer since initial enrollment.

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:30.060
The settlement with the US government requires&nbsp;
Prudential to pay out life insurance claims&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:32.760
regardless of the presence of&nbsp;
evidence of insurability form,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:34.860
as long as it had been collecting premiums&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:38.220
for that customer. This, according to a&nbsp;
statement from the Department of Labor.

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:42.780
The Department of Labor has reached similar&nbsp;
settlements with other insurers for similar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:48.420
reasons. In 2021, it settled with United of&nbsp;
Omaha Life Insurance, which had been denying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:53.100
claims payments even after the customer had&nbsp;
been paying premiums for years. In 2022,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:57.180
Reliance Standard Life Insurance came to&nbsp;
a similar settlement for a similar reason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.180 --> 00:02:01.800
with the Eighth Circuit Court of Appeals, which&nbsp;
ruled in favor of a widower who sued the insurer.

